event: response.output_text.delta
data: {"type":"response.output_text.delta","content_index":0,"delta":".","item_id":"msg_098d39c1090bbd98016a68b995c5788190b7e5e248768e3e9c","logprobs":[],"obfuscation":"dRSWJCmKFOTvYda","output_index":3,"sequence_number":422}

event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_098d39c1090bbd98016a68b995c5788190b7e5e248768e3e9c","logprobs":[],"output_index":3,"sequence_number":423,"text":"FAIL\n\n### Voice Gap 1: The central promise is framed as an unresolved possibility\n\n**Our passage:** “If it does, you'll know. And you'll put this book down and carry on exactly as before, having lost nothing. If it doesn't — if the pleasure turns out to be less than it appears, or different from what you assumed — then something remarkable becomes possible.”\n\n**Real passage:** “No doubt you find that hard to believe but read on. I have only good news for you.” / “This time, it is going to be different. Don’t expect it to be hard. I’ll explain why it isn’t going to be hard in due course.”\n\n**What Carr's voice does here:** Carr acknowledges the reader’s disbelief without sharing it. His certainty feels earned because he does not demand immediate agreement; he simply promises that the explanation will follow. This gives the reader both safety and confidence in the method.\n\n**Where our voice fails:** The explicit possibility that BAD SUGAR really does provide the special pleasure under investigation—and that the reader may therefore abandon the book—makes the chapter’s core method promise sound genuinely unresolved. The same hesitation returns in “If the examination does its job” and “if BAD SUGAR turns out to be less than you believed.” Instead of lending the reader confidence while permitting doubt, the prose adopts the doubt itself. That weakens the assigned offer of freedom from the debate and makes the book sound like a neutral inquiry rather than a guide that knows where the inquiry leads.\n\n**Initial suspicion (speculative):** framing — the investigation contract appears to have been interpreted as requiring neutrality about the method’s central verdict.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Overall voice-effect assessment:** The largest gap is weakened earned authority at the chapter’s primary promise. The repeated conditional wording—“If it does,” “If it doesn’t,” and “If the examination does its job”—turns the promised end of the sugar debate into one possible outcome among others, whereas Carr acknowledges present disbelief while remaining unequivocal that understanding will bring freedom."}

event: response.content_part.done
data: {"type":"response.content_part.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_098d39c1090bbd98016a68b995c5788190b7e5e248768e3e9c","output_index":3,"part":{"type":"output_text","annotations":[],"logprobs":[],"text":"FAIL\n\n### Voice Gap 1: The central promise is framed as an unresolved possibility\n\n**Our passage:** “If it does, you'll know. And you'll put this book down and carry on exactly as before, having lost nothing. If it doesn't — if the pleasure turns out to be less than it appears, or different from what you assumed — then something remarkable becomes possible.”\n\n**Real passage:** “No doubt you find that hard to believe but read on. I have only good news for you.” / “This time, it is going to be different. Don’t expect it to be hard. I’ll explain why it isn’t going to be hard in due course.”\n\n**What Carr's voice does here:** Carr acknowledges the reader’s disbelief without sharing it. His certainty feels earned because he does not demand immediate agreement; he simply promises that the explanation will follow. This gives the reader both safety and confidence in the method.\n\n**Where our voice fails:** The explicit possibility that BAD SUGAR really does provide the special pleasure under investigation—and that the reader may therefore abandon the book—makes the chapter’s core method promise sound genuinely unresolved. The same hesitation returns in “If the examination does its job” and “if BAD SUGAR turns out to be less than you believed.” Instead of lending the reader confidence while permitting doubt, the prose adopts the doubt itself. That weakens the assigned offer of freedom from the debate and makes the book sound like a neutral inquiry rather than a guide that knows where the inquiry leads.\n\n**Initial suspicion (speculative):** framing — the investigation contract appears to have been interpreted as requiring neutrality about the method’s central verdict.\n\n## Final verdict\n\n**Overall voice-effect assessment:** The largest gap is weakened earned authority at the chapter’s primary promise. The repeated conditional wording—“If it does,” “If it doesn’t,” and “If the examination does its job”—turns the promised end of the sugar debate into one possible outcome among others, whereas Carr acknowledges present disbelief while remaining unequivocal that understanding will bring freedom."},"sequence_number":424}

